# Long-Form Grammar

## Purpose

Keep cinematic productions behaving like films: structure flows from the
screenplay, shots earn their length, arcs pay off across the full runtime,
and delivery specs hold steady from first frame to last.

## Scope

Applies only when `production_type` in `production/project.yaml` is
`cinema` (short, feature, series). Layers on top of `rules/common/` and the
domain layers; it narrows them for long-form work, never contradicts them.

## Core Principles

- The screenplay is the structural authority. Scenes, sequences, and acts
  come from the script and story bible — never from what is convenient to
  generate.
- Runtime is a resource the story spends, not a limit to race. A shot holds
  as long as it works dramatically and not one beat longer.
- One film, one look, one delivery spec. Long-form work is judged on
  consistency across the whole runtime, not on any single frame.

## Structure and Pacing

- Every scene MUST map 1:1 to a screenplay scene (`SC_###`, per
  `writing/screenplay-format.md`); sequences group scenes by dramatic
  movement, and the edit plan MUST preserve that structure unless the
  screenplay is revised first.
- Shots MUST earn their length: each shot file's timing carries a dramatic
  justification. NEVER pad runtime with unmotivated holds, and NEVER cut
  early to imitate short-form energy.
- Coverage per scene MUST be complete enough that the editor has choices —
  master plus the coverage the blocking demands. A scene with a single
  editorial path SHOULD be flagged at shot-list review.
## Arcs, Setups, and Payoffs

- Setups and payoffs MUST be tracked in the story bible across the full
  runtime; a payoff many scenes downstream of its setup is normal in cinema
  and MUST NOT be collapsed for brevity.
- Character and prop state arcs (wardrobe, custody, injury, weather) MUST
  stay continuous across sequence boundaries — drift compounds over long
  runtimes, so continuity-state review runs per sequence, not just per scene.

## Delivery Discipline

- Exactly one primary deliverable: the `aspect_ratio`, `fps`, and
  `resolution` in `project.yaml` are locked for the entire film. Every
  keyframe and clip MUST be generated in that spec; NEVER mix aspect ratios
  or frame rates between scenes.
- The color pipeline (LOOK_* masters, grade intent in the style bible) MUST
  be consistent across the whole film; per-scene grade notes vary within one
  pipeline, never across pipelines.
- Festival/theatrical specs are set at project init and changed only by a
  human decision that supersedes `project.yaml`.

## Commercial Techniques Out of Place

- NEVER apply hook-first editing: cinema opens at the story's pace, and a
  cold open is a dramatic choice, not a retention tactic.
- NEVER generate platform variants (9:16 reframes, duration cutdowns) of
  cinema shots; `deliverables[]` arrays belong to `rules/commercial/` only.
- Sound-off readability, burned captions, and platform safe areas are not
  cinema constraints; do not let them shape blocking or framing.

## Validation

- `scripts/validate.js` checks `production_type: cinema` projects declare a
  single aspect ratio/fps/resolution and rejects `deliverables[]` arrays.
- Shot files missing timing justification and single-path scene coverage
  are flagged at shot-list review by production-qa.
- Arc and setup/payoff completeness is human review against the story bible
  at edit-plan approval.
